{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '177,227p' host/chez/records-coll.ss; sed -n '258,331p' host/chez/records-coll.ss; sed -n '1,65p' host/gambit/eval-test.ss; ls -t /Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure | head -10; /Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/usr/bin/git status --short\"","aggregated_output":";; outranks IPersistentVector / IPersistentSet / IPersistentMap, and IRecord\n;; outranks IPersistentMap (prefer-method), so a defrecord has no shape at all.\n;; Exactly these four have a print-method there — a type declaring only\n;; IPersistentList or IPersistentCollection falls through to #object[…], so neither\n;; is listed.\n;; The epoch is read BEFORE the graph is asked, and deliberately not inline in the\n;; vector: Chez evaluates arguments right to left, so an inline read would happen\n;; last and stamp answers derived from the OLD graph with the epoch of the new one\n;; — a stale entry that revalidation could never catch. Reading it first can only\n;; understamp, which costs one re-derivation.\n(define (jrdesc-derive-ifc d record?)\n  (let ((tag (jrdesc-tag d))\n        (epoch (jrdesc-ifc-epoch)))\n    (vector epoch\n            (and (not record?)\n                 (cond ((jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.ISeq\") 'seq)\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.IPersistentVector\") 'vec)\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.IPersistentSet\") 'set)\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.IPersistentMap\") 'map)\n                       ;; print-method's java.util arms: a deftype that is a\n                       ;; java.util.Map prints as a map, over its entrySet; a\n                       ;; RandomAccess one as [..] (preferred over List), a List\n                       ;; as (..) and a Set as #{..}, over its seq\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"java.util.Map\") 'jmap)\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"java.util.RandomAccess\") 'jvec)\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"java.util.List\") 'jlist)\n                       ((jch-isa? tag \"java.util.Set\") 'jset)\n                       (else #f)))\n            (jch-isa? tag \"java.lang.CharSequence\")\n            record?\n            (and (not record?) (tag-declares-coll-iface? tag))\n            ;; slot 5: declares clojure.lang.Sequential. Derived here with the\n            ;; rest because it is asked on the EQUALITY path — every (= record x)\n            ;; consults it for both operands — and answering it from the protocol\n            ;; table meant a mutex and a key-vector allocation per comparison.\n            (and (not record?) (tag-declares-sequential? tag))\n            ;; slot 6: a bare deftype's DECLARED ILookup, as (3-arity . 2-arity);\n            ;; either half may be #f. The JVM gives a bare deftype no key lookup\n            ;; of its own, so a declared valAt IS the lookup and answers for\n            ;; every key — a field-named one included, which is the whole point\n            ;; when the slot holds something valAt is there to transform\n            ;; (typed.clojure keeps a lazy thunk in one and forces it here).\n            ;; A defrecord keeps its generated field-first lookup: the JVM will\n            ;; not compile one that declares another valAt.\n            ;; Derived here, so the get path pays the vector-ref it already pays\n            ;; for the flags above rather than two string hashes per lookup.\n            (and (not record?)\n                 (let ((m3 (find-method-any-protocol-arity tag \"valAt\" 3))\n                       (m2 (find-method-any-protocol-arity tag \"valAt\" 2)))\n                   ;; -arity falls back to ANY same-named impl when no arity\n                   ;; matches, so each half is confirmed against the procedure\n                      (find-method-any-protocol tag \"equals\")\n                      (and (or record? equiv (jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.IPersistentCollection\")) #t)\n                      (and (not record?)\n                           (or (tag-declares-sequential? tag) (jch-isa? tag \"java.util.List\")) #t)\n                      (and (jch-isa? tag \"java.util.Map\")\n                           (or (not (jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.IPersistentMap\"))\n                               (jch-isa? tag \"clojure.lang.MapEquivalence\"))\n                           #t)\n                      (and (jch-isa? tag \"java.util.Set\") #t)))\n            ;; slot 13: jrec-dash-field-index's answers, \"-name\" -> slot or #f\n            (make-weak-eq-hashtable)\n            ;; slot 14: a PLAIN record — see jrec-plain? below\n            (and record?\n                 (not (jch-isa? tag \"java.lang.CharSequence\"))\n                 (not (exists (lambda (nm) (find-method-any-protocol tag nm))\n                              jrec-map-method-names)))\n            ;; slot 15: the type's ^double field flags (chez-record-dbl-tbl), or\n            ;; #f — what assoc widens a replaced field by, read here rather than\n            ;; through a string-keyed lookup on every assoc\n            (hashtable-ref chez-record-dbl-tbl tag #f))))\n;; The derived vector of the descriptor asked last, as (desc . vector). Every\n;; record op opens with jrdesc-ifc-of, and a weak-table probe is most of what a\n;; plain record's count or contains? costs; a loop over one record type asks the\n;; same descriptor every time. The pair is replaced whole, never mutated, so a\n;; racing reader sees either the old pair or the new one, both consistent; the\n;; epoch is still checked on the vector, so a hit can never outlive a change.\n(define jrdesc-ifc-last (cons #f #f))\n(define (jrdesc-ifc-of x)\n  (let ((d (jrec-desc x))\n        (l jrdesc-ifc-last))\n    (if (and (eq? (car l) d) (fx=? (vector-ref (cdr l) 0) (jrdesc-ifc-epoch)))\n        (cdr l)\n        (let ((c (jrdesc-ifc d)))\n          (if (and c (fx=? (vector-ref c 0) (jrdesc-ifc-epoch)))\n              (begin (set! jrdesc-ifc-last (cons d c)) c)\n              (let ((fresh (jrdesc-derive-ifc d (jrec-record?-uncached x))))\n                (jrdesc-ifc-set! d fresh)\n                (set! jrdesc-ifc-last (cons d fresh))\n                fresh))))))\n;; A PLAIN record is a defrecord that declares none of the methods the generic\n;; collection ops consult on a record — no count, containsKey, assoc, seq, valAt,\n;; equiv, hasheq, kvreduce, reduce, iterator… of its own, under any protocol or\n;; interface — and is not a CharSequence. That is nearly every defrecord, and for\n;; it the map behaviour is fixed: its fields in declared order, then the\n;; extension map. Clojure's defrecord compiles exactly that behaviour into the\n;; class, so count/containsKey/valAt/assoc are direct methods there; here the\n;; core dispatchers (count, contains?, seq, empty?, conj, assoc, dissoc,\n;; keys/vals, reduce-kv, reduce, into, =, hash) test this flag BEFORE their arm\n;; walk and answer from the slots, instead of walking every host arm and then\n;; asking the type's method table by name (a string-keyed lookup per method,\n;; several per op). Anything that is not plain — a deftype, a record overriding\n;; one of these — keeps the arm walk unchanged.\n;;\n;; Sound because no arm but this file's own claims a plain record (the\n;; records-coll arms below exclude one explicitly, and recfast-test.ss asserts\n;; every registry is clear of it), so answering ahead of the walk cannot preempt\n;; anything. Revalidated with the rest of the derived vector: an extend-type that\n;; adds one of these methods later bumps the protocol epoch, and the type stops\n;; being plain on its next op.\n(define jrec-map-method-names\n  '(\"count\" \"containsKey\" \"contains\" \"assoc\" \"assocEx\" \"without\" \"seq\" \"cons\"\n    \"empty\" \"valAt\" \"get\" \"entryAt\" \"equiv\" \"equals\" \"hasheq\" \"hashCode\"\n    \"kvreduce\" \"reduce\" \"iterator\" \"nth\" \"peek\" \"pop\" \"first\" \"next\" \"more\"\n    \"length\" \"charAt\" \"size\" \"isEmpty\" \"containsValue\" \"keySet\" \"values\" \"entrySet\"))\n(define (jrec-plain? x) (and (jrec? x) (vector-ref (jrdesc-ifc-of x) 14)))\n;; The impl a record type declares for METHOD (any protocol), or #f. Every\n;; collection op on a record asks this first — equality asks it for equiv and\n;; equals on both operands, hashing for hasheq and hashCode, meta/assoc/nth/count\n;; each for their own — and find-method-any-protocol answers from two string-keyed\n;; tables per call. core.logic keys its substitution maps on LVars, which declare\n;; equals and hashCode, so every map probe paid four such lookups per key compared.\n;; Keyed eq? on the name: callers pass literals, so a repeat is one eq?-ref; a\n;; name spelled by a different string object just fills its own entry.\n(define (jrec-method x method)\n;; eval-test.ss — the G3 gate for the Gambit compiler path (jolt-mj95.4).\n;;\n;; Run via `make gambiteval` (detection-gated, from the repo root). Boots the\n;; full manifest + the cross-minted :gambit seed, then drives real jolt SOURCE\n;; through jolt-compile-eval and compares the jolt-pr-readable rendering\n;; against values captured from the Chez build (bin/jolt -e \"(pr-str EXPR)\").\n;; The same rows run compiled-to-js under node (manual; see the demo recipe in\n;; the site docs) — this gate pins the gsi half.\n\n(##include \"boot.ss\")\n\n(define failures 0)\n\n(define (check src expected)\n  (let ((actual (guard (e (#t \"THREW\"))\n                  (jolt-pr-readable (jolt-compile-eval src \"user\")))))\n    (if (string=? actual expected)\n        (printf \"  ok     ~a => ~a\\n\" src actual)\n        (begin (printf \"  FAIL   ~a: got ~s expected ~s\\n\" src actual expected)\n               (set! failures (+ failures 1))))))\n\n(printf \"== jolt source through jolt-compile-eval on gsi ==\\n\")\n\n;; arithmetic + tower\n(check \"(+ 1 2)\" \"3\")\n(check \"(* 2/3 6)\" \"4\")\n(check \"(+ 9007199254740993 1)\" \"9007199254740994\")\n;; collections + rendering\n(check \"{:a 1 :b 2}\" \"{:a 1, :b 2}\")\n(check \"(conj [1 2] 3)\" \"[1 2 3]\")\n(check \"(assoc {:a 1} :b 2)\" \"{:a 1, :b 2}\")\n;; lazy seqs + HOFs through the prelude\n(check \"(map inc [1 2 3])\" \"(2 3 4)\")\n(check \"(->> (range 100) (filter odd?) (map #(* % %)) (reduce +))\" \"166650\")\n(check \"(->> (range 20) (filter even?) (map inc) (take 5) vec)\" \"[1 3 5 7 9]\")\n;; def + cross-row invocation + recursion\n(check \"(defn fib [n] (if (< n 2) n (+ (fib (- n 1)) (fib (- n 2)))))\" \"#'user/fib\")\n(check \"(fib 20)\" \"6765\")\n;; let/loop\n(check \"(let [x 10] (* x x))\" \"100\")\n(check \"(loop [i 0 acc 0] (if (< i 5) (recur (inc i) (+ acc i)) acc))\" \"10\")\n;; strings + regex\n(check \"(str \\\"jolt on \\\" \\\"gambit\\\")\" \"\\\"jolt on gambit\\\"\")\n(check \"(re-seq #\\\"[a-z]+\\\" \\\"jolt on gambit js\\\")\" \"(\\\"jolt\\\" \\\"on\\\" \\\"gambit\\\" \\\"js\\\")\")\n;; defmacro then use in a later row\n(check \"(defmacro unless2 [c a b] (list 'if c b a))\" \"nil\")\n(check \"(unless2 false :yes :no)\" \":yes\")\n;; try/catch\n(check \"(try (/ 1 0) (catch Exception e :caught))\" \":caught\")\n;; sort with comparator\n(check \"(sort > [3 1 4 1 5 9 2 6])\" \"(9 6 5 4 3 2 1 1)\")\n;; records + protocols\n(check \"(defrecord Pt [x y])\" \"#'user/map->Pt\")\n(check \"(:x (->Pt 3 4))\" \"3\")\n(check \"(pr-str (->Pt 1 2))\" \"\\\"#user.Pt{:x 1, :y 2}\\\"\")\n(check \"(defprotocol Shape (perim [s]))\" \"#'user/perim\")\n(check \"(defrecord Sq [w] Shape (perim [s] (* 4 (:w s))))\" \"nil\")\n(check \"(perim (->Sq 5))\" \"20\")\n;; multimethods\n(check \"(defmulti area :shape)\" \"#'user/area\")\n(check \"(area {:shape :circle :r 21})\" \"THREW\")   ; no method yet -> throws\n(check \"(defmethod area :circle [c] (* 2 (:r c)))\" \"#object[clojure.lang.MultiFn 0x0 \\\"area\\\"]\")\n(check \"(area {:shape :circle :r 21})\" \"42\")\n;; anonymous fns + map results\n(check \"((fn [a b] {:sum (+ a b) :prod (* a b)}) 3 4)\" \"{:sum 7, :prod 12}\")\n1.12.5\n1.11.3\n1.11.1\n1.12.0\n1.8.0\n1.11.4\n1.9.0\n1.10.3\n1.5.0\n1.6.0\nUU host/chez/records-coll.ss\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
